+# The Daily Grind
+
+The homepage includes passwordless customer registration, discoverable passkey sign-in and autofill, a profile editor, saved-passkey listing/renaming/removal, and passkey reauthentication. The existing coffee-shop content and links remain intact.
+
+## Build and run
+
+Requires Node.js 22.13 or newer (including `node:sqlite`).
+
+```sh
+npm ci
+npm run build
+npm start
+```
+
+`npm start` builds the browser bundle before starting the application. The server serves both the homepage and the account API; a static-only host cannot authenticate users. Local defaults are `http://localhost:11222` and RP ID `localhost`.
+
+Set these environment variables for deployment:
+
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin, for example `https://coffee.example.com` (no trailing slash). Required in production. Requests from other origins are rejected.
+- `RP_ID`: the registered passkey domain. Defaults to the hostname of `APP_ORIGIN`. Keep it stable; changing it invalidates access to existing passkeys.
+- `PORT`: server port, default `11222`.
+- `DATABASE_PATH`: SQLite file path, default `data/accounts.sqlite`. Its parent directory must exist. Store it on a persistent volume and back it up. This implementation is intended for a single Node server instance.
+
+Use an HTTPS reverse proxy in production and set `NODE_ENV=production`. Session cookies are HTTP-only, same-site, and secure on HTTPS. Sessions expire after eight hours; WebAuthn challenges expire after five minutes and are consumed before verification. SQLite stores public credential material, counters, profile information, and hashed session identifiers. Private keys stay with the customer's passkey provider.
+
+Profile updates, additional passkey registration, and removal prompt for verification with a credential belonging to the current account, with user verification required. The server enforces a five-minute reauthentication window. An account's last passkey cannot be removed; register a backup on another device or provider first. There is no password or email recovery route, and email addresses are account identifiers rather than verified email ownership claims. Customers who lose every passkey cannot recover access through this application.
+
+The frontend uses native WebAuthn APIs with locally bundled `webauthn-polyfills`. Signal API calls are optional and fail independently of account operations. The small local provider registry is a subset of the [passkey-authenticator-aaguids registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids); unknown/zero AAGUIDs use a neutral icon and provider name. Provider metadata is used only for display.
+
+No browser verification or development/preview server was used during implementation. Build and syntax validation do not substitute for testing with real authenticators before production deployment.
